# Line endings are pinned, they are not a matter of taste here.
#
# These scripts are edited on both a Linux and a Windows machine and run by bash
# on both. Bash treats a carriage return as part of the word it follows, so a
# script saved with CRLF does not merely look odd - it does not run at all: its
# shebang names an interpreter called "/bin/bash\r", and every "then" is
# "then\r". ytdlp.sh and lib/podcastFeeds.bash were committed that way once and
# could not be executed on Linux until they were converted back.
#
# The feed tables are here for the same reason from the other side: a row is
# tab-separated and its last column is a URL, so a stray carriage return ends up
# inside the address.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.tsv   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
